With each significant change to the codebase, add an entry to [COMMIT_MSG.txt](mdc:COMMIT_MSG.txt) describing very
briefly what was done.

The entry should be in point form, beginning with a hyphen. 

It should be in present active voice; for example, "Refactor blorbos for consistency" or "Adjust
spacing of ducks to reduce resource consumption". (Do not actually put these examples in the file!)

Entries should describe high-level goals/features that were achieved; do not add entries related to  
intermediate steps or debugging (e.g. "fix hydration error" or "edit properties in some file so that
we can continue implementing feature X"). You may add entries for debugging if it is clear that the
entire purpose/context is about a major debugging episode, e.g. "the user said that today we are
doing an overhaul of feature X and getting rid of problems" is a good reason to create an entry, but
"the user asked me to fix a problem that arose in the middle of implementing a feature" is not. 

For corrections to previous edits or additions (e.g. "I misinterpreted what feature the user was
asking for" or "the high-level description of the feature changed as we worked through its
implementation") you should not add a new entry, but may edit an existing entry *if it has become
inaccurate* due to the change.

